 mosquitoes would develop resistance  to the spraying the World Health  Organization launched a global campaign  to eradicate malaria  the dream was not to be by the late 60s  it was clear eradication was not  possible controlling malaria was a more  realistic approach snare Hawks one out  of four people it touched died  the rest were marked for life millions  were affected in 1958 countries started  massive vaccination campaigns to wipe  out the disease worldwide but to  vaccinate every man woman and child on  earth proved an overwhelming task in  1967 the World Health Organization  launched a new campaign with a different  approach contain the disease that each  outbreak stop the chain of transmission  every case had to be tracked down all  who had been in contact had to be found  unvaccinated  the search went on everywhere in schools  public places through person-to-person  contacts even financial rewards were  offered for those reporting cases the  question was asked over and over again  have you seen anyone who looks like this  [Music]  step-by-step the disease was pushed back  to the very last case  Ali Mao Molly  with an e a chain of transmission as old  as mankind was broken smallpox was no  named on the 8th of May 1980  dr. Hoffman Marla director-general of  the World Health Organization signed a  certificate of eradication the 10-year  campaign cost 300 million dollars a real  bargain the world saved each year 1000  million dollars on vaccines in carapace  it alone the saving in human suffering  has no place important as the  elimination of smoke ox was hundreds of  millions in the less developed countries  were still trapped in a vicious cycle of  poverty and disease most people had no  access to basic health care average life  expectancy hovered at 50 many mothers  died in childbirth and many children  like these died before reaching  adolescence  [Music]  something had to be done a new effective  long-term plan that would benefit  everyone and health for all by the year  2000 was the gold set of the World  Health Assembly in 1977 a year later the  where head was declared at Alma Arta in  villages such as this one a new vision  began to take root a vision based on  primary health care that is health for  people in their home of schools villages  where they live and work a vision based  on local men and women trained to care  for common health problems forty years  ago the World Health Organization was  created to help build a healthier world  where are we today world population has  reached the five billion mark remarkable  progress has been made in science and  medicine yet the health picture varies  widely in industrialized nations many  millions are invested in medical  technology but blatant inequalities in  health status remain  [Music]  high technology takes us ahead it also  takes it for example nuclear accidents  as lasting threats to in response to  these dangers groups are demanding  control over their health and  individuals are taking personal action  in the poorer nations the picture is  very different three-quarters of the  world's population received less than  10% of the world's budget for health  women have a 200 times greater risk of  dying during pregnancy and childbirth  than women in the rich nations while  these countries still face the diseases  of poverty they must learn to confront  the diseases of affluence fueled by the  adoption of Western lifestyles alcohol  and tobacco use surged upward sewed of  heart disease and cancer rates for the  first time in their history these  countries must battle yesterday's and  tomorrow's diseases at the same time  today the world is facing a grave new  threat the AIDS virus we don't know  where it came from but we do know that  it brings suffering and death  [Music]  more than half of the people diagnosed  with AIDS have died  fortunately it can only spread from one  person to the next in a few ways  sex blood and from mother to child the  AIDS virus is 